<GuideLayout
  lang="en"
  title="How to Play Google Minesweeper: Open It, Flag, Win - Meathill Minesweeper"
  heading="How to Play Google Minesweeper"
  description="Everything about Google Minesweeper: how to open it from the search page, how to flag with right-click or long-press, what the numbers mean, and where it falls short for players who want to improve."
  path="/guide/google-minesweeper/"
  keywords="google minesweeper, how to play google minesweeper, how to flag in google minesweeper, minesweeper without a mouse"
  updated="2026-09-03"
  lede="Google Minesweeper is the easter egg built into search: type “minesweeper” and a playable board appears at the top of the results, with rules identical to classic Minesweeper. Here is how to open it, how the gestures work, and when to reach for a fuller version."
  faq={faq}
  howTo={howTo}
>
  <h2>How do you open Google Minesweeper?</h2>
  <p>
    The fastest way: <strong>search for "minesweeper" on Google</strong>. A playable board is embedded at the top of the
    results — click and play. It comes from Google's easter egg / Doodle series: no app to install, no account to sign in.
  </p>

  <h2>What are the rules?</h2>
  <p>Identical to classic Minesweeper:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Numbers</strong>: an opened number counts the mines in the 8 cells around it.</li>
    <li><strong>Flagging</strong>: mark cells you know are mines to protect against misclicks.</li>
    <li><strong>Safe first click</strong>: like most modern implementations, your first click never hits a mine.</li>
    <li><strong>Winning</strong>: open every non-mine cell to win; the page tracks your time.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How do you flag in Google Minesweeper?</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Right-click a cell on desktop</strong>; <strong>long-press a cell on mobile or tablet</strong>.
    Flags matter beyond preventing misclicks: when the flags around a number equal the number itself, its remaining
    neighbors are all safe.
  </p>
  <p>
    Chording — opening those remaining neighbors at once — is triggered by double-click in most Minesweeper
    implementations. Where a version lacks the gesture, mid-game slows to one cell at a time, which is a big reason
    players eventually move to dedicated Minesweeper sites.
  </p>

  <h2>Playing without a mouse (trackpad users)</h2>
  <p>
    On a laptop trackpad, "right-click" usually means two-finger click, and chord-style "press both buttons" is
    miserable. Two options:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Live with system gestures: two-finger tap for right-click, and pay for it in speed.</li>
    <li>Switch to a trackpad-first version: <a href="/">Meathill Minesweeper</a> drops right-click entirely — when flags
      match a number, double-click it to open the rest, tuned for single-button Apple mice, Mac trackpads and phones.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Where Google Minesweeper falls short: fun to play, hard to learn</h2>
  <p>As a casual distraction it is great. For players trying to improve, three things hurt:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>No <strong>probability hints</strong>: coin-flip moments stay coin flips, and you never learn how close the call was.</li>
    <li>No <strong>replay</strong>: a loss is just over — you cannot revisit the turning point.</li>
    <li>No <strong>saved stats</strong>: times and win rates do not persist across devices, so progress is invisible.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If that matters to you, play a round of <a href="/">Meathill Minesweeper</a>: Learn Mode overlays a live probability
    heatmap, scores every decision, and the post-game replay chart plots efficiency and mine probability at real click
    times — turning guessing into calculating.
  </p>
</GuideLayout>
